.TH ESDM\-EBPF\-COLLECT 8 "2026-08-05" "ESDM @VERSION@" "ESDM Manual"
.SH NAME
esdm\-ebpf\-collect \- collect raw event deltas of the eBPF entropy sources
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B esdm\-ebpf\-collect
.B \-\-source
.RB { sched | irq }
.B \-\-out
.I FILE
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B esdm\-ebpf\-collect
obtains raw, unconditioned noise source data from the eBPF based scheduler and
interrupt entropy sources
.RB ( es_sched_ebpf ", " es_irq_ebpf ),
for an SP800\-90B entropy assessment. It serves the same purpose for those
sources as the
.I esdm_raw_*
debugfs interfaces of the
.I linux_esdm_es
kernel add\-on do for the kernel based ones.
.PP
The tool loads and attaches its own copy of the eBPF programs, so every
invocation starts from a freshly initialized source: zeroed per\-CPU state, no
previous time stamp and an unlearned timer granularity. That is what makes a
restart test possible \- one run is one row.
.PP
It must be run as root, and it refuses to run when the kernel FIPS mode is
enabled. It is built only with
.BR "\-Des_ebpf_testing=enabled" ,
which must never be set for a production build. The ESDM server itself never
records raw data.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-source " " { sched | irq }
Entropy source to collect from.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-out " \fIFILE\fR"
Output file, one delta per line.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-events " \fIN\fR"
Stop after
.I N
deltas have been written. Default: 10000000. Since the count is of deltas
actually written, an interrupted collection yields exactly the requested
number and losses show up as a longer run rather than a shorter assessment.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-cpu " \fIN\fR"
Write only the deltas of CPU
.IR N .
Without it the deltas of all CPUs go into one stream in arrival order, which
is handy to look at but mixes what are separate noise sources and cannot be
split again \-
.B do not assess that stream.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.SH OUTPUT FORMAT
One decimal time delta per line \- the input format of
.BR esdm\-extractlsb (8).
A delta is the time between two events of one CPU, with the timer granularity
already divided out, exactly as the ESDM server does it, so that the assessed
signal is the one that actually enters the entropy pool. The undivided time
stamps cannot be collected: they report close to zero entropy on coarse clock
sources, notably the monotonic clock in virtualized environments, whose
low\-order bits are constant.
.PP
The time stamps are read with
.BR bpf_ktime_get_ns (),
the same source the deployed entropy sources use. The entropy rate must
therefore be measured in the environment the deployment actually runs in, as
the clock resolution differs notably between bare metal and virtualized
systems.
.PP
The deltas are formed in the eBPF program, always between two events one CPU
observed back to back, and each is handed over on its own. Nothing user space
receives can therefore span a gap: a record the ring buffer had no room for is
a sample that never arrives, not a delta stretched across the ones it
swallowed. Two events yield no delta at all \- the first one a CPU sees, and
the first one after a reset.
.SH EXAMPLES
Collect a million deltas of CPU 0 from the scheduler source:
.PP
.RS
.nf
esdm\-ebpf\-collect \-\-source sched \-\-cpu 0 \-\-events 1000000 \\
                  \-\-out sched_idle_cpu0.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Turn them into the byte stream the NIST tools assess and run the estimator:
.PP
.RS
.nf
esdm\-extractlsb sched_idle_cpu0.txt sched_idle_cpu0\-8.bin 1000000 FF
ea_non_iid sched_idle_cpu0\-8.bin 8
.fi
.RE
.PP
The scripts
.I analyze_non_iid.sh
and
.I analyze_restart.sh
next to the tool do the whole procedure for one CPU. Collect at least 1,000,000
deltas per CPU, once on an idle system and once under load, and take the
minimum across all CPUs and load profiles.
.SH NOTES
From the measured per\-event min\-entropy
.IR H ,
the entropy rate is
.I floor(H * 256 / 64)
\- an event contributes its 64 bit delta, so 256 bits of data are four events \-
and the oversampling rate is
.IR "ceil(1 / H)" .
The two have to agree: the accounting credits the smaller of what each yields
and caps a rate above
.IR "4 / OSR" .
Configure them at build time
.RB ( \-Des_sched_ebpf_entropy_rate=N ,
.BR \-Des_sched_ebpf_osr=N )
or the rate at runtime through the ESDM configuration interface. The shipped
default rate of 0 keeps the source uncredited until this measurement has been
performed for the deployment environment.
.PP
The full procedure, including the SP800\-90B restart test, is described in
.I addon/es_ebpf_testing/README.md
of the ESDM source distribution.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR esdm\-extractlsb (8),
.BR esdm\-getrawentropy (8),
.BR esdm\-server (8)
.SH AUTHOR
Markus Theil <theil.markus@gmail.com>
